{
  "teamId": 109,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Arizona Diamondbacks were one of the most difficult teams to watch in recent franchise history, finishing with a 52-110 record that ranked among the worst in baseball. After a surprisingly respectable 15-12 April that offered a glimmer of hope, the wheels came off catastrophically in May and June, when the team went a combined 8-48 and effectively ended any realistic conversation about competitiveness before summer had even arrived. The bullpen was the constant culprit, surrendering home runs and big hits at the worst possible moments, turning close games into lopsided losses night after night.\n\nThe few bright spots in the season's high-leverage moments were largely accidental or fleeting. Josh VanMeter's three-run blast in late April offered a rare moment of joy, and a couple of relievers — Ryan Buchter and Joakim Soria — somehow found themselves on the right side of big moments in July, a month when the team managed a modest 11-12 record and looked almost respectable again. But those were islands in an ocean of futility. The pitching staff posted a 5.11 ERA as a whole, and the parade of arms yielding grand slams, walk-off shots, and back-breaking extra-base hits became a defining image of the year.\n\nAs the summer wore on into August and September, exhaustion set in. The roster was cycling through fringe arms — names like Noé Ramirez, J.B. Wendelken, Tyler Clippard, and Brandyn Sittinger — all of whom found themselves on the wrong end of crushing home runs. Even Madison Bumgarner, the veteran anchor of the rotation, gave up a Jose Altuve bomb in September that summarized the helplessness of the second half. The offense, posting a .691 OPS, simply could not bail out a rotation and bullpen that hemorrhaged nearly 900 runs across the season.\n\nThe 2021 Diamondbacks were a franchise in transition, absorbing a painful rebuild year that offered little in the way of redemption. The month-by-month record told the story plainly: one decent April, two months of near-historic losing, and then a grinding, joyless crawl to the finish line. The silver linings were thin — young players like Daulton Varsho got experience, and the organization would eventually use this painful season as fuel for a remarkable turnaround in the years ahead. But in 2021, there was little to celebrate in the desert.",
